    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Business Bank Statement Loans: How to Qualify for a Mortgage When Your Tax Returns Don't Tell the Full Story</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you own a business, pay yourself from it, and your bank account reflects a financial position that looks nothing like what your tax returns report, a business bank statement loan was built specifically for your situation. This is not a niche workaround. It's one of the most widely used Non-QM mortgage products for small business owners.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What a Bank Statement Loan Actually Does Differently</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A conventional mortgage qualifies you on documented net income from your tax returns. A business bank statement loan qualifies you on your actual business deposits over the prior 12 to 24 months. The lender applies an expense ratio to those deposits to estimate your net qualifying income and uses that number for debt-to-income calculations instead of what your Schedule C or K-1 reports. For business owners who have maximized legitimate deductions, the deposit-based number is often significantly higher than the tax return number, sometimes by a wide enough margin to change the entire qualification picture.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Why the Expense Ratio Matters as Much as the Deposits</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">The lender doesn't use 100% of your deposits as qualifying income. They apply an expense ratio that estimates what portion of those deposits represents actual business overhead. The remainder is treated as available income.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">A standard expense ratio for many lenders is 50%, meaning half of your total deposits are counted as qualifying income. Some lenders use lower ratios for businesses with documented lower overhead, which increases your qualifying income further. If your business is a professional services firm, a consulting practice, or any operation with relatively low hard costs, you may be able to document a lower expense ratio and qualify for more.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The expense ratio your lender uses directly determines your qualifying income, and lenders vary in how they handle it. Ask about it explicitly before you commit to an application.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How Business Bank Statement Loans Are Structured</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Most programs require 12 or 24 months of business bank statements, with 24 months generally producing more favorable results because it smooths out irregular months and demonstrates sustained deposit history. You'll need to show that the deposits are consistent with business operations, not one-time transfers or non-recurring events.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">You'll typically need to document that you own the business, provide a business license or CPA letter confirming self-employment, and show that the account reflects genuine business income rather than personal transfers from other accounts.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Rates on bank statement loans run higher than conventional rates, reflecting the reduced documentation and the Non-QM designation. For borrowers whose only alternative is a conventional qualification that doesn't get them to the purchase price they need, that premium is often the right tradeoff.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Pull 24 months of business bank statements and calculate your average monthly deposits.</strong> Apply a 50% expense ratio as a starting estimate. Multiply by 12 for an annualized qualifying income figure. Compare that to what your tax returns show.</li>
      <li><strong>Identify whether your business has documented lower overhead that might support a reduced expense ratio.</strong> If your actual business expenses run significantly below 50% of revenue, ask lenders whether they'll consider a lower ratio with supporting documentation.</li>
      <li><strong>Make sure your bank statements reflect clean, consistent business deposits.</strong> Large irregular transfers, non-business deposits mixed in, or significant month-to-month volatility can create underwriting questions. Know what your statements show before your lender reviews them.</li>
      <li><strong>Compare 12-month and 24-month qualification scenarios.</strong> If your income has grown, 12 months may show a higher average. If a strong year two years ago pulls the average up, 24 months may be stronger. Run both before you decide which to submit.</li>
      <li><strong>Get rate quotes from more than one Non-QM lender.</strong> Bank statement loan rates vary more across lenders than conventional rates do. Shopping two or three lenders costs you nothing and can produce a meaningful difference in your monthly payment.</li>
    </ol>

